{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Achashdarpanim.</b> A Persian word of nine letters, and there is no epithet in Scripture like it in its numerical pattern, and the words of those who say they are two words do not seem right to me." "<b>HaPachot</b> is missing the tav of the root, and the correct view is that the one is pechah with a heh, [and] pechat Yehudah is construct state, and the word pechat [means] like 'their pechah being in the land of Yehudah' — like one passing through the marketplace by the pechah, and the mem is a plural marker."], :book :esther, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "Resolved the untranslated Hebrew lemma head to a transliteration of the Aramaic/Hebrew form.", 1 "Preserved the compressed syntax of the original, which strings together multiple clauses without explicit connectives; the argument pivots on distinguishing pechah (governorship) from pechat (construct form)."}, :source-witnesses [["Kol Sason, Krotoschin, 1840" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001728315"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:30:02.962679373Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}